.\" debark-gui.1 -- the Debark desktop application.
.\"
.\" Hand-written man(7), not generated. debark's own pages come out of
.\" `debark gen-manpages`, which walks the cobra command tree it already
.\" has (internal/cli/cmd_genmanpages.go). This program has no command tree
.\" and no flags at all, so there is nothing to walk and nothing a generator
.\" could produce that is not already written here. See docs/packaging.md.
.\"
.\" Every claim below was checked against the code or against the running
.\" binary; nothing was copied from docs/user-guide.md. Where something could
.\" not be established it is left out.
.TH DEBARK\-GUI 1 "2026-09-07" "debark\-gui" "Debark Manual"
.
.SH NAME
debark\-gui \- Debark, a desktop front end for debark
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B debark\-gui
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Debark
prepares software transfers for machines with no network connection. It runs
on the online builder machine: name the offline machine as a target, pick
packages from a catalogue built out of that target's own apt indexes, add
vendor
.I .deb
URLs or local files, and build a bundle \- a flat apt repository plus a signed
manifest \- to carry across.
.PP
The window is titled Debark; the executable and the package are named
.BR debark\-gui .
.PP
It is a front end and decides nothing itself. Every resolve, fetch, build,
signature and verification is performed by running
.BR debark (1),
which is located by searching
.B PATH
and then the directory holding this executable. Without it the application
still starts, and its first screen explains what is missing. The catalogue is
the one thing this program reads apt data for directly, and it reads it only
to show a list: what actually goes into a bundle is resolved by
.BR debark ,
by asking the target release's own apt.
.PP
Debark runs on the builder machine only. Nothing about the offline target
changes: over there it is still
.BR debark ,
or plain apt.
.
.SH OPTIONS
None. This program takes no options and ignores any arguments it is given \-
there is no
.B \-\-help
and no
.BR \-\-version .
The command line belongs to
.BR debark (1):
anything Debark can do is expressible as a
.B debark
command, and the build screen shows the exact command it is about to run
before it runs it.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
This program is a window, not a job. Its exit status says how the process
ended, never how a build turned out; a failed build is reported in the window,
and the process still exits 0 when the window is closed.
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination. The window was closed, or the process was sent
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
both of which are handled and shut the application down.
.TP
.B 1
The application could not be started. The reason is printed to standard error
as \fBdebark\-gui:\fR followed by the message.
.TP
.B 2
The process was terminated by an unrecovered panic, whose message and
goroutine trace are printed to standard error. On a correctly installed
package the reachable cause is a failure to initialise GTK \-
\fBpanic: failed to init GTK\fR \- which is what happens when there is no
display to open.
.PP
.BR debark (1)
has a frozen table of exit classes 0 to 7. It describes
.BR debark ,
not this program, and none of it reaches this program's status: the class of a
failed
.B debark
run is carried into the message the screen shows instead.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The child
.B debark
processes inherit this application's environment unchanged, so
.BR debark (1)'s
own variables apply to every build started here. Listed below are the ones
this program reads itself, plus the two that most visibly change what a build
started from this window does.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for the
.B debark
executable. If it is not there, the directory holding
.B debark\-gui
is searched next, and the readiness screen names both places.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where the catalogue cache is written, and where the webview writes its own
cache. Defaults to
.IR ~/.cache .
See FILES.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the webview stores website data, and where
.BR debark 's
package store lives by default \- the location this application's disk\-space
check measures. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where this application looks for the operator signing key, under
.IR debark/ .
Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B DEBARK_CONFIG
.BR debark 's
configuration file. When it is set, the signing key is looked for beside it \-
in the directory holding that file \- rather than under
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.TP
.B DEBARK_SIGN
The signing key
.B debark
is configured to use. Read here so that the readiness screen reports the key
.B debark
would actually sign with, rather than a key only this application knows about.
.TP
.B DEBARK_SELF_BINARY
Path to a Linux build of
.B debark
for the container backend to mount into the container and re\-execute. Read
here for the readiness row that reports it, and by
.B debark
itself. It matters on Windows, where the
.B debark
on
.B PATH
is a
.I .exe
that cannot run inside a Linux container; the same need is met without any
setting by a
.I bin/debark\-linux\-<arch>
binary sitting beside the
.B debark
this application runs.
.TP
.B DEBARK_STORE
Overrides where
.BR debark 's
package store lives, and therefore what the disk\-space check measures.
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Not read by this program, and passed straight through to
.BR debark ,
where it fixes the timestamps written into a bundle so that two builds of the
same inputs can be compared. A value that is not a non\-negative base\-10
count of seconds is refused by
.BR debark ,
so a stray one in this application's environment fails every build it starts.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the one temporary file a running build needs is created: an NDJSON
stream
.B debark
writes its events into, named
.IR debark\-events\-*.ndjson ,
removed when the build ends.
.TP
.BR USER ", " USERNAME ", " LOGNAME
Read only to fill in the account name in a command the readiness screen
displays for the operator to copy. Nothing is decided from them.
.PP
GTK and WebKitGTK read a large number of variables of their own. They are not
this program's and are not documented here.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/debark-gui/catalog/
The catalogue cache: one directory per target, holding the packed package
index and a small header. This is the disk an operator reclaims and the
directory to remove to force a rebuild. Deleting it is safe at any time, with
the application running or not \- the next visit to that target rebuilds it,
and nothing installed anywhere is decided from it. The layout is specified in
.I docs/dev/cache-format.md
in the source repository.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/debark-gui/WebKitCache/ ", " $XDG_CACHE_HOME/debark-gui/CacheStorage/
The webview's own cache, created by WebKitGTK when the application starts.
Also safe to delete.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/debark-gui/
The webview's website data \- an HSTS database and the local storage the
chosen colour theme is remembered in.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/debark/operator.key ", " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/debark/operator.pub
Where this application looks for an operator signing key, and where the
readiness screen's "create a signing key" action proposes writing one. The
files are written by
.BR "debark keygen" ,
never by this program. The
.I .pub
half has to reach the offline machine by some route other than the media the
bundle travels on; a public key carried alongside the bundle proves nothing.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/debark/store/
.BR debark 's
package store. Not written by this program, but it is what the disk\-space
readiness row measures and where the finished downloads of a stopped build
remain.
.PP
Everything else this program writes goes where the operator named it: the
bundle output folder chosen on the build screen, and the destination chosen on
the export screen.
.
.SH NOTES
Debark needs no root and no other elevated privilege at any point, installs no
service and no system configuration, and never opens a block device.
.PP
There is no telemetry, no crash reporting, no analytics and no update check.
The only hosts contacted are the distribution archives named in the target's
own sources and vendor
.I .deb
URLs the operator entered; there is no endpoint operated by this project for
it to contact.
.PP
A command shown for the operator to copy has any credentials in a vendor URL
removed from it, so it describes the build faithfully but may need the
credential supplied again before it will run.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR debark (1)
.PP
The operator's guide \- what the application is for, the four steps it takes
you through, and what to do when a check fails \- is
.I \%docs/user\-guide.md
in the source repository,
.BR \%https://github.com/inferops/debark\-gui .
